// PENDING (conservative proof gap -- valid code WRONGLY REJECTED, found
// 2026-07-08). A sum PAYLOAD field declared with a range (`case One(v: i32
// [0..=50])`), destructured in its matching arm (`P::One { v } -> use_v(v)`) and
// passed to a same-ranged parameter, FAILS to prove: "cannot prove transition
// argument `self.p.v` satisfies bounded parameter `x`; expected 0..=50". The
// destructured payload binding does not carry the payload field's declared
// range, so a same-ranged param/arithmetic use cannot be proven.
//
// SOUND to fix (verified): construction STORE-ENFORCES the payload range --
// `P::One { v: 100 }` is rejected ("value 100 is outside its declared range
// 0..=50") -- so a read of `self.p.v` inside the `P::One` arm is provably in
// [0..=50]. This is NOT a miscompile; it just refuses valid proof-carrying code.
//
// ⚠️ THE OBVIOUS FIX IS UNSOUND (proved 2026-07-08). The arg proof resolves
// `self.p.v`'s range via omega-proof/obligations.rs data_field_in_definition
// (guard-BLIND: obligation-building has no case context per-expression).
// Extending it to search variant payloads makes THIS repro pass -- but the same
// resolution feeds DIRECT payload access outside a case-arm, which is currently
// admitted (`let got = self.p.v`). Direct wrong-case access is a CHECKED partial
// op, NOT a silent misread: native TRAPS (exit 132/ud2), interp ERRORS ("case
// `Two` carries no payload field `v`", exit 101) -- both detect it, they just
// surface the trap differently. So the field's "case is active" obligation is
// UNDISCHARGED (exactly analogous to an index bound). The unconditional range is
// then a proof LIE: it claims a total in-range value where the program can trap
// (`case Two(w:[0..=1000])`; `p=Two{w:900}; got:[0..=50]=self.p.v` COMPILES
// falsely with the change, is correctly REJECTED without it, traps 132 at run).
//
// THE SOUND FIX (scoped 2026-07-08) is a CHECKER-side feature, not an
// obligation-building edit. A case-dispatch arm's guard desugars to tag
// arithmetic (see memory match-desugar-tag-arithmetic-interp): `P::One { v } ->`
// carries `guard = When(self.p.tag == One's ordinal)`, already on the
// BoundedTransitionArgumentObligation. The fix: in the checker's transition-arg
// range resolution, when the argument is a payload member `self.p.v` AND the
// arm guard proves `self.p`'s tag == the ordinal of the variant that OWNS `v`,
// admit `v`'s declared payload range (reuse guard-narrowing). It MUST live in
// the checker (which has the guard); obligation-building resolves the field
// range guard-blind, so an unconditional extension there is the unsound path.
// Multi-fire (needs tag-ordinal -> variant -> payload-field mapping). Direct
// access outside an arm has no case-proving guard, so it stays unproven (sound),
// and separately warrants its own compile-time fence (a design call: make the
// undischarged case obligation loud instead of a runtime trap). See memory
// sum-payload-range-not-propagated for the full trace.
//
// Exits 20 when the fix lands (P::One { v: 20 } -> use_v(20) -> exit 20).
boundary trait Console { machine exit_process(return_code: i32); }
data P { case One(v: i32 [0..=50]); case Two(w: i32 [0..=50]); }
data Main { console: Console; p: P; }
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.p = P::One { v: 20 };
    transition self.p {
        P::One { v } -> use_v(v)
        P::Two { w } -> use_v(w)
    }
    state use_v(&mut self, x: i32 [0..=50]) { self.console.exit_process(x); }
}
machine Main::run(&mut self) { self.main(); }
